# Dependencies — ask first

## Context

`app/package.json` has exactly two devDependencies (`typescript`, `vitest`) and
zero runtime dependencies. That is the point of the exercise: the store, the
text helpers, and the tests are all in-house. Every "just add a small package"
suggestion quietly removes the constraint the rules exist to protect.

## Rule

- NEVER run `npm install`, `npm add`, `yarn add`, or `pnpm add`, and never edit
  the `dependencies`/`devDependencies` blocks of `app/package.json` on your own
  initiative.
- Solve it with the standard library and what is already here first: the custom
  store for state, `app/src/lib/text.ts` for strings, vitest for tests.
- If a package is genuinely required, stop and propose it: name, why the
  in-house option cannot work, install size, and what it replaces. Wait for a
  yes before touching `package.json`.
- `app/package-lock.json` is only ever regenerated by an approved install —
  never hand-edit it.
- The same applies to new tooling configs (eslint, prettier, babel, jest): this
  sample has no linter configured on purpose. Say so instead of adding one.

## How to verify

1. `git diff app/package.json app/package-lock.json` — empty, unless an
   approval for the specific package is in the conversation.
2. `grep -c '"' app/package.json` aside, `dependencies` is still absent and
   `devDependencies` still lists only `typescript` and `vitest`.
3. `grep -rn "from \"[a-z@]" app/src` — every bare import resolves to `vitest`
   or a Node builtin; everything else is relative.
4. No new config files at `app/` root (`.eslintrc*`, `.prettierrc*`,
   `jest.config.*`).
